!# ls.pdxdoc -- man-equivalent for `doc ls` back-end.
!# Grammar: design/tooling/plan.md I7 + doc repo's PdxdocParser
!# (M1-002). Parsed line-oriented; `!doc` header exactly once;
!# `!section` boundaries between top-level sections; `!posix-diff`
!# and `!ref` and `!example`/`!end-example` are passthrough at M1
!# of the `doc` reader, first-class from doc M2 onward.
!doc ls
!version 1.0.0

Directory listing that emits typed `PdxFsDirEntry[]` records.

Reads a directory's entries via the paideia PdxFS substrate and
renders each entry as text to the terminal AND as a schema-typed
record on the semantic pipe (stdout as `KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT`).
Downstream consumers (`grep`, `find`, `pipeviz`) can filter or plot
by field name rather than by column-slicing text.

!section synopsis

  ls [-l] [-a] [-h] [--json] [--schema] [--color=<v>] [<path>]

`<path>` defaults to `.` when omitted. Positionals beyond `[0]` are
ignored at 1.0 (multi-path listing lands at 1.1). Unknown flags
return exit 2.

!section flags

  -l              Long format: kind mode owner size mtime name.
                  [parsed; not yet wired into the read loop -- see
                  note below]
  -a              Include dot-prefix (hidden) entries. [live]
  -h              Render sizes as base-2 K/M/G/T/P/E.
                  [parsed; consumed only by the unwired -l renderer]
  --json          Emit each row as one JSON object on stdout.
                  [parsed; no consumer in this repo yet]
  --schema        Print ls's declared output schemas + exit 0.
                  [parsed; no consumer in this repo yet]
  --color=<v>     `<v>` in {auto, always, never}. Colors are
                  driven by the entry's declared schema/MIME, NOT
                  by POSIX file-type bits. See POSIX-diff below.
                  [parsed; renderer shipped, not yet wired]

At 1.0 the read loop honours only `-a` directly. `-l`, `-h`, and
`--color=` are recognised by the argv parser and their renderers
(`LongFormat`, `HumanSize`, `ColorPicker`) are shipped and tested,
but `Runner::runner_ls` does not call them yet, so passing these
flags today produces output byte-identical to bare `ls`. `--json`
and `--schema` set their flag bits with no consumer at all. This is
deliberate: the argv surface, the exit-code map, and the 144-byte
wire body are frozen at 1.0, so wiring a renderer into the read loop
is an additive change on the 1.x line, not a re-sign. The `-l`/`-h`
examples below show the shipped renderer's actual byte output for
when it is wired in; the `--schema` example is a proposed output
shape with no implementation behind it yet (tracked as `ls.ENH-006`).
None of these examples are reproducible by running `ls` at 1.0.0.

The nine standard flags (`--help`, `--version`, `--schema`,
`--json`, `--color=`, `--dry-run`, `--verbose`, `--quiet`,
`--no-cap:<name>`) are recognised by `libpdx-argv`'s StdVocab and
land under the uniform I3 vocabulary at 1.0.

!section capabilities

ls receives exactly four capabilities at exec, per `caps.decl` at
the repo root:

  KIND_USER                              -- invoker identity for
                                            the owner column and
                                            the DirListRecord tail.
  KIND_TTY(write)                        -- terminal write; no
                                            read, no tcsetattr.
  KIND_PDXFS_FILE(read, <arg-path>)      -- narrowed to the
                                            argument path only;
                                            no ambient authority.
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT                      -- the semantic-pipe
                                            stdout sink for the
                                            PdxFsDirEntry stream.

Missing caps -> exit 4 (cap denied). Extra caps refused at the
shell's `cap_manifest_verify` before ls ever runs.

!section semantic pipe -- PdxFsDirEntry@0.1

Each accepted entry emits a 144-byte wire record on stdout:

  bytes 0..127     128-byte kernel record (inode, kind, name_len,
                    name[0..103]).
  bytes 128..143   16-byte owner Cap wire: KIND_USER at offset
                    128, user_row placeholder at offset 136 until
                    `sys_pdxfs_stat_by_inode` ships (owner_row
                    lookup lands at 1.1; the wire slot is
                    reserved today so a 1.1 record is byte-
                    compat with a 1.0 consumer).

The 32-byte schema-hash prefix is added by `libpdx-semantic-pipe::
Send::send_record`; the ls compose path itself produces bytes
0..143 only. Golden for the compose byte range lives in
`tests/schema_golden.pdx`.

!section audit

`DirListRecord` opened via `libpdx-audit` BEFORE the first
`KIND_TTY` write; committed at the Runner epilogue. If the audit
broker is unavailable, ls exits 3 (system error) without emitting
any user-visible bytes. This is audit-first per invariant I5.

!section exit codes

The 0xFFFFEBxx internal band folds to the shared I4 vocabulary at
`src/exit_map.pdx`:

   0  success (including "directory was empty").
   2  usage error (bad flag, malformed path, missing).
   3  system error (audit broker down, IPC bind failure, other
      unclassified upstream failure).
   4  capability denied (missing cap, substrate refusal, cap
      slot not populated).

The three exit-code rows the M4-003 fixture pins as invariant:

  empty=0        directory has zero entries; no error.
  missing=2      path was ill-formed at the ls side, OR the
                 shell refused to hand ls a cap.
  cap-denied=4   substrate returned -EBADF or -EACCES on
                 readnext.

!section colorization

`--color=` is driven by the entry's declared schema/MIME, not by
POSIX file-type bits. This is a Paideia D2 literal -- the color
picker consults the entry's schema handle first, then falls back
to the MIME registered on the file's `.pdxfs` metadata, then
finally to a `KIND_UNKNOWN` neutral color. The M4-001 fixture
pins 10 dispatch cases + 3 SGR byte-exact goldens for the
palette.

!posix-diff

POSIX `ls -F` and `ls --color` render decoration from the
file-type bits in `st_mode` (regular / directory / symlink /
socket / FIFO / block / char). Paideia has no such bits at the
filesystem layer -- every entry carries a schema handle instead.
`ls --color=` therefore looks the same shape as POSIX color but
answers a different question: what is this entry *for*, not what
kind of inode is it. A directory rendered blue in POSIX may be
rendered red in Paideia if its schema is a repository root, and
a `.pdxsig` file may be rendered green if its schema is a signed
manifest -- the color follows the schema, not the mode bits.

!posix-diff

POSIX `ls -l` renders the owner column as a text uid resolved
through `/etc/passwd`. Paideia has no `/etc/passwd` -- users are
capabilities. `ls -l` renders the owner column as `u:<row>`
where `<row>` is the row index in the founder-provisioned user
table (per R48). Consumers who want the human-readable name
pipe through `caps decode --owner-col` (a post-1.0 tool at
`caps` M2+).

!section examples

!example
$ ls
argv_surface.pdx
runner.pdx
semantic_emit.pdx
!end-example

!example
$ ls -l
- 644 u:0 12103 ns:1755808140000000000 argv_surface.pdx
- 644 u:0 22163 ns:1755841640000000000 runner.pdx
- 644 u:0 27731 ns:1755870660000000000 semantic_emit.pdx
!end-example

Columns are `kind mode owner size mtime name`, single-space
separated with no column padding. `kind` is one letter from the
PdxFS-v1 type nibble (`-` regular file, `d` dir, `l` symlink, `s`
special, `?` unrecognised); `mode` is three raw octal digits (no
POSIX `rwx` glyph translation until the M3 schema/MIME palette
lands); `mtime` is `ns:<decimal>`, the raw nanosecond epoch value,
since calendar rendering awaits pdx-time (R54) -- pipe it through
a schema-aware consumer to get a calendar date.

`-l` clusters with no other short flag at 1.0 (`parse_argv` rejects
clustered short forms -- exit 2); pass each short flag separately.

!example
$ ls -l -h
- 644 u:0 11.8K ns:1755808140000000000 argv_surface.pdx
- 644 u:0 21.6K ns:1755841640000000000 runner.pdx
- 644 u:0 27.0K ns:1755870660000000000 semantic_emit.pdx
!end-example

`-h` renders the size column as `N.dK`/`N.dM`/... (base-2, one
decimal digit) via `HumanSize::human_size_render`; it never rounds
to a whole unit, so 12103 bytes is `11.8K`, not `12K`.

!example
$ ls --schema
declares_output_schemas:
  - PdxFsDirEntry@0.1
!end-example

`--schema` is PROPOSED, not shipped: `AS_BIT_SCHEMA` is parsed but
has no consumer anywhere in this repo at 1.0.0, so the flag is
silently ignored and `ls --schema` produces the same bare directory
listing as plain `ls`. The example above is the target shape (`ls.
ENH-006`).

!example
$ ls | grep --schema PdxFsDirEntry --field name --pattern '.pdx$'
argv_surface.pdx
runner.pdx
semantic_emit.pdx
!end-example

